      "snippet": {
        "text": "Our study underscores the value of using benchmark data as a central strategic tool for guiding leadership decisions and continuous school improvement (Blanc et al., 2010;Marsh et al., 2012), especially in contexts where timely, objective measures of progress are crucial for targeted interventions and resource allocation (Day et al., 2016). However, relying solely on achievement data to increase student achievement would be overly simplistic. While benchmark testing provides valuable insights, it represents only one dimension of academic growth. \n\nRather, we advocate a comprehensive approach to evaluating student opportunities and success, which considers broader data sources alongside benchmark assessments and focuses on holistic development (Drake, 2024;Park, 2018). This approach aligns with Darling-Hammond's (2020) assertion that effective student learning depends not only on academic skills but also on social-emotional competence, well-being, and identity development -factors critical to fully engaging students in the learning process. Teachers' qualitative observations, for instance, offer valuable insights into how students interact with learning materials and their preferred learning methods, which is often missed by standardized tests (Ho, 2022). While not always perfect indicators, data on student behavior, attendance patterns, and overall school climate can provide useful context for understanding academic performance (Gage et al., 2016) and identifying students at risk of leaving school early (Bowers & Zhou, 2019), highlighting the importance of developing equitable policies (McIntosh et al., 2018) and policies that address root causes (e.g., Kirksey & Gottfried, 2021). Social-emotional assessments also complement benchmark data by revealing emotional intelligence, interpersonal effectiveness, and problem solving in social contexts (Herrenkohl et al., 2020). \n\nBy integrating benchmark test data with these additional indicators, educators can gain a more comprehensive understanding of student needs. This holistic approach enables educators to tailor personalized strategies to better address each student's unique strengths and challenges. Furthermore, addressing these interrelated aspects of student development may foster a more supportive and equitable educational environment. In this context, future research should explore the interplay between academic metrics and broader indicators of learning to better support the whole child (Flores & Gunzenhauser, 2021).",
