      "snippet": {
        "text": "The key insight from this analysis pertains to teachers' and students' beliefs, perceptions and literacy regarding formative assessment.In terms of teacher beliefs and perceptions, formative assessment is viewed as an effective tool to enhance teaching and learning.This includes providing immediate feedback on students' progress [17,18,36], developing students' self-assessment literacy and self-directed reflection during online learning [19], designing peer tutoring programmes to improve oral fluency and reading comprehension [52] and using electronic portfolios to reflect on the learning process [48].We agree that teachers' and students' attitudes and practices are crucial factors in formative assessment in K-12 EF L education.In our experience, educators who see formative assessment as a tool for learning are more likely to adopt it to adjust their teaching methods to meet the needs of students.They view mistakes as learning opportunities and value the process of student growth over time, rather than just focusing on the final grades.Conversely, if teachers believe that assessments are solely for grading purposes, they may not fully engage with the formative aspects of assessment.They may overlook the potential of formative assessment to provide insights into student understanding, identify learning challenges and tailor instruction accordingly.\n\nPositive beliefs can encourage teachers to use formative assessment more effectively, whereas negative beliefs may lead to a superficial implementation of assessment.For instance, some teachers believe that the entire process of formative assessment is tedious and view it as a waste of both teacher and student time [34].This lack of familiarity with formative assessment may stem from insufficient training, whether pre-service or in-service [35].Furthermore, experienced teachers may be more inclined to use formative assessment because they recognise its importance in customising instruction and meeting the diverse needs of students.Contrarily, some novice teachers may feel uncertain about how to effectively design and implement formative assessment and may therefore rely on traditional summative assessment methods.This is demonstrated by the minimal amount and substandard quality of teachers' feedback to students, wherein teachers continue to focus on identifying errors to justify their grades for student essays [3,28].\n\nTeachers' perceptions of formative assessment are complex and diverse.They recognise the multiple potential benefits of formative assessment.",
