        "text": "Language assessment has witnessed a widening scope of research ranging from highstakes tests to low-stakes classroom contexts. However, attention to classroom-level practices necessitated adopting a sociocultural perspective that views assessment as an integral part of pedagogy and instruction (Shepard, 2019). Assessing student performance in the classroom has always been an indispensable part of teaching and learning, yet the systematic study of classroom assessment has been recently on the agenda. \n\nDistinguishing between traditional and performance-based assessment procedures, i.e. summative (assessment of learning) at one end of the continuum while formative (assessment for learning) at the other, has long been discussed. However, recently the distinction between these forms has been replaced by the arguments that propose the synergy of them (Black & Wiliam, 2018). Classroom assessment, therefore, has been conceptualised as \"both formative assessment, used to adapt instruction and help students to improve, and summative assessment, used to assign grades\" (Shepard, 2019, p. 183). Because instructional policy documents analysed in this study embraced performance-based/alternative and traditional assessment as the terminology (see e.g. Ministry of National Education [MEB], 2014), we adopted these terms to refer to classroom assessment practices. \n\nUnderstanding classroom assessment as the combination of traditional and performance-based assessment procedures provided attention to all the practices used in the teaching and learning procedure. Hill and McNamara (2012) conceptualised classroom assessment practices in a continuum ranging from product-oriented measures such as multiple-choice testing to performance-based tools like tasks, portfolios, and self/peer assessments as well as unplanned assessment practices such as eliciting answers from the students. Such a broader conceptualisation of assessment maintained the inclusion of a diverse range of data including the teacher's decision-making to understand assessment practices in diverse classroom contexts. Different from traditional assessment procedures evaluating merely students' achievement of learning, assessment tools, either summative or formative, are used as a source of feedback to improve teaching and learning within the classroom assessment paradigm (Black & Wiliam, 2018). More recently, Brookhart and McMillan (2020) have proposed a revised definition of classroom assessment as follows:",
