        "text": "The study was conducted in cooperation with a Dutch center for school counseling, HCO, located in The Hague. All 222 schools in the region of this center were invited, through a written letter, to participate in the study. Within three weeks, 45 schools had agreed to participate. These schools were located in The Hague and surrounding cities. At an informational meeting for school principals, the research procedures and the expectations of the school were explained. The randomization procedure, the testing load, and the types of tests to be administered were discussed. In addition, it was made clear that the experimental schools were not allowed to use their own reading comprehension curriculum for the duration of the study and that the regular program was to be completely replaced by the experimental program. None of the schools withdrew after this meeting. We randomly assigned schools rather than classrooms/teachers to the two treatments to prevent treatment diffusion within schools. Because of the length of the intervention, cooperation between teachers was likely. An independent researcher from Radboud University performed the randomization.\n\nAll schools were informed about their group assignment during a school visit just prior to the summer holidays. Despite our efforts to keep all schools on track, five schools withdrew at the beginning of the study (four control and one experimental), due primarily to internal management problems within the schools. Just prior to the final posttest one experimental school and one control school withdrew because they were unable to organize the testing due to changes in location, that is, moving to different buildings. The available data of the last two schools were used in the analyses. We had, however, no data from the five schools that dropped out already at the beginning; this is a relatively low attrition rate but differential attrition was high (18% of the control schools and 4.3% of the intervention 