      "snippet": {
        "text": "Although this review offers a comprehensive overview of English as a second language for formative assessment in K-12 education, it has certain limitations.First, although this review identified various facets of formative assessment, the presented list of factors is not exhaustive.There could be other formative assessment practices that have not yet been explored empirically.Second, some pertinent literature may have been overlooked despite an extensive search.By limiting our focus to peer-reviewed, high-quality publications, we may have missed valuable insights from other sources such as book chapters and conference papers.In addition, the process of analysing and interpreting literature involves subjective judgement, which could affect the understanding and interpretation of data.Despite efforts to remain objective and impartial, personal biases and theoretical inclinations may be unavoidable.\n\nFuture research should investigate the long-term effects of formative assessment on students' learning achievement and motivation and ways to sustain and consolidate these effects.As educational technologies continue to evolve, studies should explore in depth how technology can be effectively integrated into formative assessment, including the use of artificial intelligence, gamified learning and adaptive learning platforms.Moreover, future research should focus on teacher-student interaction in formative assessment, including how to enhance students' active participation and enable teachers to effectively guide and support students' self-evaluation.",
