      "snippet": {
        "text": "Applying a random-effects model, the overall mean effect was extracted followed by subgroup analyses on moderators contributing to the overall mean effect size. \n\nWith the random-effects model, the overall mean effect size for the between-group studies was medium (g = 0.684, SE = 0.26, CI 95 = 0.18, 1.18). The overall mean effect size for the within-group studies was large (g = 1.39, SE = 0.67, CI 95 = 0.08, 2.69), indicating generally medium to large effects of English language intervention on struggling readers learning English as a foreign language in South Korea. Due to the nature of the within-group study design, the mean effect size was likely to be larger than that of the between-group studies. The subgroup analyses were additionally conducted and have the following implications. \n\nFirst, when affective and achievement types were categorized within the outcome measures, the affective type-such as confidence, motivation, interest-showed large effects along with other achievement type measures such as phonological awareness, speaking, vocabulary acquisition, and writing. As the affective measures considered essential in evaluating the students' learning performance and serve as an important quality set of criteria for struggling readers (Park & Park, 2007), this type of outcome measure should be more assessed and encouraged to be used as a quality indicator. The findings may indicate that those variables, such as students' confidence, participation, positive attitude towards English learning, should receive more attention as intrinsically motivating factors, not just the achievement results alone. For instance, an intervention study on grammar in the low-achieving foreign language classroom and found that the learning was very much mediated by student-teacher perceptions of learner autonomy and motivation (Graus & Coppen, 2017). The results of this currently study underscore the importance of those affective factors influencing the students' performance with large effects. Such affective measures beyond the achievement measures should be given more attention for enhanced learning experience, bottom-up approach, and positive atmosphere in the English language intervention setting. \n\nSecond, in the intervention analysis, due to different variables measured in the between-group and withingroup studies, the k number quite varied.",
