      "snippet": {
        "text": "Regarding the intervention method, the interactive instructional method that was bottom-up (student-toteacher) not top-down (teacher-to-student) approach had the largest effect size, and the difference was significant. Dependent variables of achievement measure subsetsletter recognition, phonemic awareness, phonics, fluency, vocabulary, reading comprehension, and combination type did not play as a significant moderator to the mean (Jung & Choi, 2019). Lee and Chang (2017) meta-analyzed on the studies of the instruction effectiveness on low-achieving elementary school students and found that the overall mean effect size of English language instruction for low achievers was large (d = 1.32); the teacher-student reading intervention method showed the largest effect (d = 1.78) followed by technology usage (d = 1.61). When the intervention lasted for 9-16 weeks, the effect size was the largest (d = 1.58) for the duration. \n\nIn the secondary school at the middle school level, the overall effects of English language instruction on the underachievers with 7 moderating variables such as publication type, school type, region, treatment period, and dependent variables like linguistic and affective measure domain were analyzed (Lee, Chang & Jeon, 2017). The results showed that the overall mean effect for the struggling readers in secondary school was large (d = 1.24). It was also found that studies conducted in small cities had a larger effect than those in metropolitan areas. Of moderating variables, the effect size in grammar was larger than in other linguistic skills and knowledge; the effect sizes in attitude and students' interest within the affective outcome measure were larger than in others (Lee, Chang & Jeon, 2017). \n\nIn the presence of effectiveness of strategized direct and explicit teaching, some findings indicate some challenges with Response-to-Intervention (RTI) implementation with English language learners (Orosco & Klingner, 2010) as evidence-based interventions do not apply to all students, and the impact of interventions on those students is still not so clear especially when they require special education or if they are already struggling readers with their first language.",
