      "snippet": {
        "text": "Based on a conventional definition of feedback as consequential to performance (Hattie & Timperley, 2007) and that performance on the objectives of instruction is captured through evaluative assessments (Bloom, 1968), there is a strong procedural sequence in which feedback follows assessment, independent of whether the purpose of assessment was summative or formative. This definition exploits the control engineering concept of feedback as something that happens consequential to a setting in the system (Bennett, 1979). Hence, feedback in this paper consists of information to students after assessment events or processes. This distinguishes from other notions about feedback such as using feedback instructionally as cues for students as to their instructional needs or opportunities in classroom contexts (Konold et al., 2004;Pollock, 2012) or using feedback as a system of learning (Molloy & Boud, 2014). \n\nFeedback involves giving learners information about the task, the learning process, or the metacognitive self-awareness students have about the instructional objectives and their own learning in relation to those objectives (Hattie & Timperley, 2007). Feedback occurs in multiple formats (e.g., grades, scores, commentary, re-instruction, etc.;Ruiz-Primo & Li, 2013). In learning-oriented contexts, formative feedback provides information that supports learning at a time point when change is still possible. What makes it formative is that the learner has a chance to change their work in response to the feedback and gain deeper learning or better achievement. In contrast, feedback from summative assessments normally provides overall summative merit evaluations (e.g., passfail, A to F, or score) without further opportunity to revise or change. Hence, feedback given by teachers in classroom contexts is almost always formative and quite often based on informal assessments and interpretations. An important function of assessment, regardless of format or timing, is to provide information to teachers about students' knowledge and skills in relation to the curricular objectives (Black & Wiliam, 2009). Formative assessment requires the collection of information about student progress, difficulties, and successes to improve teaching or learning (Black & Wiliam, 2009;Bloom et al., 1981).",
