      "snippet": {
        "text": "Formative assessment has long been recognized as an effective approach to improving student learning outcomes. Formative assessment, when applied consistently, can significantly improve learning achievement (Black & Wiliam, 1998). Analysis of 30 articles supports these findings, showing that formative assessment strategies make an important contribution to developing students' conceptual understanding, Luthfi Ilham Ramdhani/ Enhancing Student Learning Outcomes through Formative Assessment: A Systematic Literature Review critical thinking skills, and learning motivation at various levels of education. The application of the formative-summative evaluation model in mathematics learning provides significant improvements in student learning outcomes, with statistical analysis showing a high strength of effect (Gaspersz & Suranto, 2023). This finding is in line with previous findings that formative assessment in engineering learning not only improves learning outcomes but also builds an active learning community (Sanchez-Lopez et al., 2023;Savinykh, 2022). One of the key components of successful formative assessment is specific, relevant, and actionable feedback. Collaborative dialogue between teachers and students creates a supportive learning environment (Ramos dos Santos et al., 2021;Zelenska et al., 2022). The feedback provided in the formative assessment process helps students understand areas for improvement, while encouraging self-regulation in learning (Clinchot et al., 2017). Self-efficacy theory also emphasizes the importance of positive and specific feedback in increasing students' confidence in their ability to achieve learning outcomes (Hanim et al., 2022;Saraswati et al., 2021;Soares et al., 2021). \n\nIntegrating formative assessment into the Assessment for Learning framework is a strategic approach to creating continuous learning. Formative assessment not only serves as a tool for measuring student performance but also encourages their engagement through feedback tailored to individual needs. This approach supports the development of lifelong learning competencies, where students actively reflect on their progress and identify strategies to improve learning outcomes. For example, in the context of language learning, teachers can use the portfolio method to help students evaluate their skills independently, providing a real picture of the application of Assessment for Learning.",
