        "text": "Research and expert practice indicate two guidelines for facilitating young children's mathematics learning. First, synchronize personal and digital activities (e.g., Dahshan & Galanti, 2024;Galen & Buter, 1997;Hutinger & Johanson, 2000;Sarama & Clements, 2020b). Second, for both these types, engage children at their developmental level for each mathematics topic and monitor and support their progress using formative assessment (e.g., Jung & Conderman, 2015;Konca et al., 2023;Tazouti et al., 2023;Thai et al., 2022). Further, both components of formative assessment, the assessment and the linked adaptive instruction, benefit from a combination of high-and low-tech approaches. The Bajillions system will coordinate and combine low-tech instructional activities of personal, face-to-face experiences with high-tech digital activities targeted through embedded assessment grounded in validated pedagogy (Clements, Lizcano, et al., 2023). Such ongoing and deliberate use of formative assessments to monitor progress and adjust instruction helps all children learn (Black & Wiliam, 1998;Connor et al., 2018;Gallego et al., 2018;National Mathematics Advisory Panel, 2008;Penuel & Shepard, 2016;Shepard et al., 2018;Thomson et al., 2005). Unfortunately, most early childhood teachers lack the time and training to assess mathematics accurately and link results to instruction (Kilday et al., 2012). Also, unfortunately, although digital technologies could reduce the assessment burden on teachers, most apps do not use formative assessment, especially along research-validated developmental progressions (Callaghan & Reich, 2018). \n\nThe Bajillions system will address this problem because it conducts formative assessments of each child's level of thinking within key LTs and provides detailed information about specific topics that need particular. Further, teachers' observations of children playing the activities and reflections on the resulting reports will support teachers' use of low-tech curriculum-embedded assessments built into every personal activity. \n\nThe importance of directly linking planning, instruction, formative assessment, and feedback cannot be overstated (Jung & Conderman, 2015).",
