        "text": "The results provided evidence that a mixture of traditional and performance-based assessment practices were outlined to assess student achievement in upper secondary education. Yet, a strong wish for using nontraditional components in assessment was presented in the documents; while the policy was moving from top to down, several tasks (e.g. e-portfolio, tech pack) and feedback sources (self and peer) were incorporated into the program. The policy directives then seemed to embrace the communicative approach that shifted assessment practices from assessing content through traditional formats to assessing the process of language skills (Nunan, 2022). \n\nIn this regard, policy directives for classroom assessment revealed several practices that can improve day-to-day learning and teaching. However, the implementation of the policy indicated some inconsistencies; sometimes assessment was used as a negative reinforcement instrument, and sometimes for classroom management, which is not desired considering the pedagogical principles embraced in classroom assessment (Black & Wiliam, 2018;Shepard, 2019). \n\nFor instance, using plus or minus to assess performance-based tasks, as well as inefficient use of rubrics to assess speaking performance demonstrated ineffectiveness in the classroom-level realisation of the policy. According to Smith (2016), exposure to assessment directives via policy documents can result in a strong yet technical application of the policy at schools; teachers can incorporate assessment practices into their teaching without understanding the underlying principles and pedagogy. Similar findings were reported by Tian et al. (2023) in the Vietnamese tertiary-level EFL context. \n\nAnother critical issue in classroom-level assessment practices was the lack of effective feedback. While various feedback sources, such as peer, self, and teacher feedback, have been proposed, performance-based assessments in classroom settings often failed to 