        "text": "We summarize here a multi-study process used to revise and test the Developing Talkers curriculum supplement. A previous random assignment study of Developing Talkers conducted in 2010, found positive intervention effects on at-risk students' proximal receptive vocabulary learning (d ¼ 0.81), no effects on a proximal listening comprehension task, and some evidence of an increased rate of proximal vocabulary learning for Tier 2 students relative to a comparison group of at-risk students. Although this pilot study of the first four-week version of the curriculum showed positive correlations between teachers' fidelity of implementation and child outcomes, it did not utilize the completed, more extensive version of the curriculum or consider possible effects of using a research-based curriculum supplement on teachers' instructional quality. After this promising 2010 pilot study, we developed a 12-week intervention (version 1.0) that was utilized in a statewide PD program from 2010 to 2014 in more than 2,500 classrooms. In this article, we describe feedback that indicated that a fully revised intervention (version 2.0) could improve quality, feasibility of implementation, and teacher learning supports. In the present studies, we explain how this new version was iteratively developed from 2014 to 2016. We hypothesized that the revised version would have positive impacts on teachers' instruction and, thereby, student outcomes.\n\nThe design and sequence of the present studies were informed by Clements' (2007) Curriculum Research Framework in which 10 phases of development and evaluation guide the iterative design and culminate in a scalable and research-proven curriculum product. Our process pursued a more rapid focus on feasibility for at-scale use (Glennan, Bodilly, Galegher, & Kerr, 2004) because version 1.0 of the curriculum was already being used in a sizeable capacity. Our work for Clements' phases 1 through 4 which established the a priori foundations and learning model that guide curriculum development was summarized in the introduction to this article. Thus, Study 1 below was conceptualized as a market analysis study consistent with phase 5 of the Clements (2007) framework. Studies 2 and 3 fall into the formative phases of the framework, where we evaluated usability and feasibility (phases 6-8). Study 4 describes a pilot study that initiated the summative phase (phase 9) of the Developing",
