        "text": "To be effective, our intervention had to be perceived as feasible to implement by a variety of novice and expert teachers across a large state who all serve students at risk for future academic and reading difficulties. These four studies used a variety of approaches to examine the extent to which teachers serving high-need students implemented the Developing Talkers curriculum with fidelity. Some Tier 1 components such as preteaching vocabulary, vocabulary elaborations during SBR, and comprehension instruction had relatively high implementation. Others such as Tier 2 small-group lessons and vocabulary review after Tier 1 SBR were implemented less uniformly. Consistently, the greatest reported barrier to implementation was lack of instructional time. Yet, we surmised that implementation of before and during reading vocabulary instruction with adequate fidelity was the factor driving the robust child outcomes on taught words.\n\nContrary to expectations, intervention teachers did not appear to transfer the research-based strategies used during Developing Talkers to another SBR of an informational genre text. Developing Talkers and control teachers were no different on three ratings of research-based practices for vocabulary instruction, extended inferential conversations, and effective SBR. For the classroom management scale, the control teachers performed above treatment teachers. There are many possible explanations for the lack of teacher transfer effects. First, it is possible that the 26-week program featuring 13 informational genre texts was not of sufficient duration or intensity to impact the ways teachers talked about informational texts, which are known to be a less familiar genre in early childhood (e.g., Pentimonti, Zucker, Justice, & Kaderavek, 2010). Second, it is probable that teachers faced too many competing demands for instructional time and their attention; for example, these Tier 1 supplemental lessons were only allocated 20 minutes during the language arts block in which teachers were required to also implement their core curriculum. Third, it is possible that randomization was not equivalent and, without a pretest measure for control teachers, we cannot know whether they started with higher initial levels of research-based instruction. Other troubling potential explanations are that use of the Developing Talkers curriculum somehow hindered teachers' skills in managing children's behavioral expectations during SBR. In terms of the lack of transfer of explicit vocabulary instruction and conversation supports, it is possible that use of a scripted curriculum may have an unintended consequence of eliciting",
