        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "A meta-analysis of single-subject designs is essential to better understand students with reading disabilities and effective interventions for them. Single-subject design studies include participants with critical reading difficulties compared to large groups of participants with different abilities in group design studies. Group-design studies primarily target large groups of students who may not have reading difficulties. For example, Suggate (2016) examined 71 studies, of which only 15 investigated students with reading difficulties. Thus, relatively few meta-analyses have investigated technology-based interventions for students with reading disabilities, of which none are single-subject design studies. \n\nThus, this meta-analysis aims to analyze the most recent (2010-2022) technology-based reading interventions for elementary students (grades K-5) with reading difficulties. This meta-analysis also evaluated the methodological quality against the CEC (2014) standards to assess whether the effect varied according to the methods used. This review's findings will assist professionals in choosing and structuring reading interventions, offering projections of student progress to optimize their achievements. For scholars, the research underscores domains for methodical duplication and pinpoints gaps necessitating further inquiry. \n\nIn addition, potential moderators will be examined, including setting, grade level, learning disability identification, technology type, targeted skills, and Council for Exceptional Children [CEC] standards). The moderators have the potential to influence intervention outcomes significantly. Studies have pointed out several variables that positively affect intervention outcomes, which help guide intervention practices and inform educators. One of the most repeated variables is setting, which is considered due to the distinctive dynamics of individual versus group learning environments in technology interactions (Elbaum et al., 2000;Marulis and Neuman, 2010), especially for students with reading difficulties, where one to one setting appears to be more effective than group settings. \n\nStudents' grades are also considered important moderators and critical variables in response to reading interventions (Cheung and Slavin, 2013b;Scammacca et al., 2015). A large part of the curriculum for younger students (K-5 grades) is reading, compared to older students in more advanced subjects, which may give valuable insight into technology-based intervention. Additionally, the distinction between students in K-3 and 4-5 is based on educational research that suggests different developmental phases in reading skills acquisition.",
